qx|A2|st||rh||ah|Board 2 IMPs   -   April 10, 2024|pn|hondo717,danpsych,IraCT,hondo717|
md|4SHQ98DAT643CAQJ93,SKQJ8432HAJ43D98C,S97HT72DKQ5CKT862,SAT65HK65DJ72C754|sv|n|
mb|P|mb|1D|mb|1S|mb|2C|mb|?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|East must take a bid. The |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=lott,Law of Total Tricks^*N|CP||FP||at| bid is 3@S which would be fine. There is another convention which I recently started playing that also would work here. It is a little complicated but it works as follows.|

n#|9|at|^^^fIf you do follow the link you can return  HERE  by clicking the  |fp|1|n#|3|at|@L|fp||n^|3|at|   icon button, top left.|
PG| ***** 34 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|East has several cue bids available. They are 2@D, 3@C, and 3@D. I now play that 2@D shows a 3-card limit raise, 3@C shows a 4-card limit raise, and 3@D (a jump cue) shows a 7-9 4-card raise (which is called a "mixed raise"). |
PG| ***** 35 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The bid I would make if playing that convention would be 3@D, although 3@S is fine, since the 3@D convention is not very common.|
PG| ***** 36 ***** |


mb|3D|an|7-9 4 card raise (jump cue)|
pg| ***** 37 ***** |


mb|?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|My hand just got enormous over the 2@C bid. My hand's |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=dummypoints,Dummy Points^*N|CP||FP||at| are 11 HCP, 2 length points, 1 quality suit point, plus 5 points for the spade void with 5 trump (the 5 points is probably a little high but the idea is that the spade void is wonderful). Regardless, my hand is worth 18 or 19 points enough to bid game opposite the minimum of 10 that partner is showing.|
PG| ***** 38 ***** |


mb|5C|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|5S|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|West made an excellent 5@S call. Normally it is not a good idea to bid ^*B5 over 5^*n. In fact the bridge saying ^*BThe 5 level belongs to the opponents^*n is one that is not followed often enough.|
PG| ***** 39 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|5 level decisions are among the most difficult bidding decisions in bridge. With the club void West has a wonderful hand and with ^*BFavorable Vulnerability^*N (EW not vulnerable, NS are) the 5@S bid is even better because voids are so wonderful, I might even bid 5@S at equal vulnerability (e.g. if both teams were vulnerable, or neither team was.).|
PG| ***** 40 ***** |


mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|P?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Now, we are at what we call a ^*BForcing Pass^*n situation. That means when you bid game to make (which I did) and the opponents bid over that game, you should never let them play the hand undoubled. You must either double or bid one higher.|
PG| ***** 41 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In this case, although slam is possible from my perspective I can't risk it. So since North passed (e.g. didn't double or bid 6@C) that is called a ^*BForcing Pass^*n. It means I cannot pass. I must either bid 6@C or double.|
PG| ***** 42 ***** |


mb|D|mb|P|mb|P|mb|P|
pg| ***** 43 ***** |


