%%%%%%Hand 4
https://tinyurl.com/235swsw8
qx|A4|st||rh||ah|Board 4 IMPs   -   April 10, 2024|pn|hondo717,IraCT,danpsych,aspiring|
md|2SQ8643HQ97DJTCAK9,S5HJT853DA7543C32,SAKJ9HK4DKQ82CJ87,ST72HA62D96CQT654|sv|b|
mb|P|mb|1N|mb|P|mb|2H|mb|P|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Let me discuss the concept of ^*BSuper Accepts^*n over transfers. If responder transfers to a major and the 1N opener has 4-card support opener knows they have a 9+ card fit and should do what is called a ^*BSuper Accept^*n. |
PG| ***** 77 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The simple and most common way to play super accepts is for North, in this situation, bid 3@S rather than 2@S. Some play you only super accept with 4 card support and a 17 or good 16 NT hand. |
PG| ***** 78 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|However, I like to ^*BSuper Accept^*n with any strength 1N bid. There is also a more ^*BSophisticated Super Accept^*N that we will play here so I can demonstrate it. In that style, if you have 4-card support, you bid your doubleton. So here, North would super accept by bidding 3@H, which shows 4 spades and a doubleton heart. |
PG| ***** 79 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|On occasions, that will give enough additional information to responder to decide whether or not to bid game or slam. So let's have North bid 3@H.|
PG| ***** 80 ***** |


mb|3H|mb|P?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|We have game for sure but not slam. So I can bid game. Another convention is I could ^*BRetransfer^*n by bidding 4@H. But I like to play 4@H by me is a control bid looking for slam. |
PG| ***** 81 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Now you will note the problem of not having North bid 3@S is that we wrong side the contract particularly if we only want to play 3@S. |
PG| ***** 82 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|However, I think that is worth the cost in order to play ^*BSophisticated Super Accepts^*n because wrong siding the contract is often an overrated problem.|
PG| ***** 83 ***** |


